cards: build a boot card from its type; the lan card boots Alpine diskless

A card boots known-good FCPM code on any machine. `machines/cards/build TYPE /dev/sdX sdX` erases a
card and lays out its type: Ventoy first (its key is what Secure Boot trusts), then the type's
partitions. A window on the screen shows the card and waits for an OK; the home's passphrase is asked
in a window too.

- `steward`: the 256G card's recipe, out of the Buffalo's linux-persist and into git: Debian XFCE
  live with persistence, SystemRescue, Alpine; home, origin, toolbox.
- `lan`: leans on the LAN. Alpine diskless from RAM, so a slow card reader only slows the boot. Its
  kit (FAT, readable anywhere) holds Alpine's package cache, the saved settings (`lbu commit`) and
  Claude Code; `lan/kit` makes or refreshes it on any Linux. The checkout sits outside the encrypted
  home, so a job can start before anyone unlocks it. tty1 asks the home's passphrase, then logs in.

Not yet: the checkout follows GitHub until digitization offers git on the LAN; the steward recipe
has not been rerun from here.

